00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:15.750
And what that phenomenon of fandom is to us as human beings.

265
00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:17.350
It's a great conversation.

266
00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:18.070
Yes.

267
00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:26.630
And in the Mike Schur way that he is, he just has a way of understanding people and what makes

268
00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:27.210
us human.

269
00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:28.910
He's a great observer.

270
00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:29.590
Yes.

271
00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:30.150
Yes.

272
00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.650
And so we just can't wait for you to hear it.

273
00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:34.150
Well, why don't we take a break?

274
00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:39.070
We will save our chit chats and around the town for our next episode because we thought

275
00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:42.070
we were going to chat with Mike for about 25, 30 minutes.

276
00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:44.870
You guys, we talked for an hour, but it's so good.

277
00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:46.130
And you're going to love it.

278
00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:47.210
We want you to hear it all.

279
00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:49.710
So we'll take a break and we'll be right back with Mike Schur.

280
00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:00.760
Hello.

281
00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:01.980
Here we go.

282
00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:03.380
Hi, Mike.

283
00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:05.240
I am so happy to see both of you.

284
00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:05.920
How have you been?

285
00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:06.700
How is everything?

286
00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:08.560
We're good.

287
00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:09.820
How are you?

288
00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:10.480
I'm great.

289
00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:11.700
It's so great to see you.

290
00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:13.080
You look literally the same.

291
00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:14.800
I was thinking the same thing about you.

292
00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:16.440
I don't know if you're supposed to say that to people.

293
00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:21.020
No, I look, we all are aging exactly the same rate one day per day.

294
00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:25.520
And when I see people from the office, I think like, yeah, that looks, that's how the person

295
00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:25.800
looks.

296
00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:30.480
Like if you've known the person continuously, they just stay the same in your brain.

297
00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:31.520
But you know, it's crazy.

298
00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.140
So I was cleaning my SAG award the other day.

299
00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:35.100
You were what?

300
00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:37.560
First of all, incredible way to start a sentence.

301
00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:38.660
I know, right?

302
00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:40.200
I've never cleaned mine.

303
00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:40.640
I don't know.

304
00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:41.540
I was dusting.

305
00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.320
You've never cleaned your SAG award?

306
00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:44.440
I had an idiot.

307
00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:47.040
Did you not consult the manual for proper maintenance?

308
00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:47.860
I haven't.

309
00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:49.520
And also, it has a tush.

310
00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:50.520
So, you know.

311
00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:50.900
It does.

312
00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:52.040
It has, anyway.

313
00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:54.620
Is it like taking care of an infant a little bit?

314
00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:55.400
I don't know.

315
00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:56.900
I haven't thought about it.

316
00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:57.840
Go on, Jenna.

317
00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.680
I was moving things on my bookshelf in my office.

318
00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:06.860
And, you know, so I was cleaning my SAG award and I looked at the date on it and it said

319
00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:07.960
2006.

320
00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:09.800
And I was like, wait.

321
00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:12.960
That was 20 years ago?

322
00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:13.820
20 years ago.

323
00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:14.360
What?

324
00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:14.840
Yeah.

325
00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:15.600
I know.

326
00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:19.400
We're passing all those milestones now where you see things on social media that's like

327
00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:21.460
the 25th anniversary of something.

328
00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:22.860
And it's hard to believe.

329
00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:23.420
But yeah.

330
00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:26.880
I mark this every June 1st because I moved to LA on June 1St.

331
00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:29.180
1st, 2004 to start working on the show.

332
00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.980
And so every June 1st, I'm like, oh, wait.

333
00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:33.560
How many years has it been that I've lived here?

334
00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:36.060
And now it's 22 years that I've been here.

335
00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:40.480
We started working on the show, I think, like right after July 4th weekend, maybe, the

336
00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:41.780
writers met for the first time.

337
00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:45.040
And that is inconceivable to me.

338
00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:47.940
I mean, it feels like both forever ago and also yesterday.

339
00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:49.280
Well, listen.

340
00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:55.140
The last time you came on the show, we talked a lot about, like, how you got your job on the

341
00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:57.740
show and how you became Moe's and all those things.

342
00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:02.620
But we didn't get a chance to dig into some of the specific episodes that you wrote.

343
00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:03.100
Great.

344
00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:03.740
Let's do it.

345
00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:06.060
So we were thinking maybe we could talk about a few.

346
00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:11.120
So speaking of the early days, the Alliance, it's season one of The Office.

347
00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:16.280
And you guys are trying to establish what is the American version of this.

348
00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:16.620
Right.

349
00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:17.960
How do we make it our own thing?

350
00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:20.500
Can you take me back to that time?

351
00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:21.120
Yeah.

352
00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:26.720
So that first season, it was just me, Mindy, and BJ were the only full-time writers.

353
00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:30.880
And then the late Lester Lewis, RIP, was a consultant.

354
00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:32.260
And Larry Wilmore was a consultant.

355
00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:33.940
And Paul Lieberstein was a consultant.

356
00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:36.660
And it was a very small crew.

357
00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:45.520
And in classic Greg Daniels fashion, we came up with 270 episode ideas to make five episodes.

358
00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:51.800
And I want to say the Alliance, one of the things that is going on in the first season

359
00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:57.160
of The British Office is the threat of downsizing and the threat of consolidation and a branch

360
00:12:57.160 --> 00:12:58.260
closing and that sort of stuff.

361
00:12:58.340 --> 00:12:59.560
It sort of runs in the background.

362
00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:03.820
And Greg wanted to maintain that because it was and is such a part of corporate life that

363
00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:06.620
you're always like, is someone going to buy our company and are we all going to lose our

364
00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:06.980
jobs?

365
00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:12.680
And so he wanted that theme to run through the season in a low-grade way.

366
00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:19.680
And the simple nugget of the idea was that in this kind of vague threat of something bad

367
00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:25.280
happening in The Office, that because Survivor was such a big deal at the time, like people

368
00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:28.540
forget, like Survivor had debuted, I think, in the year 2000 or something.

369
00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:31.020
And around 2004, it was like the biggest deal.

370
00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.240
It still is a huge deal, but it was the biggest deal.

371
00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:39.540
And I want to say it was BJ, my memory might be failing me, who pitched the idea of just

372
00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:43.000
Dwight asking Jim to form an alliance with him, Survivor style.

373
00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:45.300
And we liked that idea.

374
00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.800
Obviously, like Jim and Dwight, famous antagonists.

375
00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:53.000
But the breakthrough was that someone pitched, I'd like to take credit for it, but I don't

376
00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.360
know if I can, that Jim's response was, absolutely, I do.

377
00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:57.300
Like instantly.

378
00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:01.960
I was going to ask you about that because that's like such a famous couplet that comes

379
00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:02.600
back later.

380
00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:04.660
It was absolutely, I do.

381
00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:10.760
And then the talking head that comes after it is, anytime Dwight asks me to do anything,

382
00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:11.920
my response is absolutely.

383
00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:20.220
Because Jim's sole goal in life in that moment is to like enjoy this mundane business environment

384
00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:21.580
in any way he possibly can.

385
00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:22.980
Just escape the boredom.

386
00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:23.920
Just escape the boredom.

387
00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:28.460
And it's one of the great lessons of writing that I've learned a hundred thousand times,

388
00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:31.020
which it's a classic improv thing, right?

389
00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:32.920
It's like, yes, and it like, just say yes.

390
00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:34.400
If you say no, the story ends.

391
00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:35.780
If you say yes, you get a story.

392
00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:40.900
So as soon as we came up with the idea that Dwight makes an absurd request of Jim and Jim's

393
00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:44.860
immediate response is yes, then, okay, we're off and running.

394
00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:48.140
So that was the like simple nugget of the story.

395
00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:55.720
And then it became like, how does Jim enjoy like torturing Dwight and enjoying his life

396
00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:58.960
in this boring day and sharing it with Pam, of course.

397
00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:04.980
My favorite scene that came out of it is the one that's sort of a spy scene from up top,

398
00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:06.780
looking down at them in the parking lot.

399
00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:08.360
And they're standing outside.

400
00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:13.140
And it was, as it always was in the valley, 111 degrees.

401
00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:16.740
But it was supposed to be like November in Scranton.

402
00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:21.500
And so the two of them just are grabbing their arms in the classic pose of person.

403
00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:22.540
I'm cold.

404
00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:27.280
Which we would then do 50 more times over the course of the first couple seasons.

405
00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:33.500
And having that like private kind of survivor-like conversation about like, you know, how to play

406
00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:35.480
things and what they've learned and all that stuff.

407
00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:36.800
So that was it.

408
00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:41.120
It was what made that show so special to me was like, that's what you needed for a story.

409
00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.500
You didn't need a gigantic, huge plot.

410
00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:49.260
You needed one of the ridiculous characters to make a request of a sane character and have

411
00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:51.620
the sane character decide to go along with it for the day.

412
00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:52.440
And that was it.

413
00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:55.240
That's a really great way to look at The Office now.

414
00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:59.340
Like the next time I watch an episode, I'll be like, okay, who's the ridiculous character

415
00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:01.180
asking the sane character to do something?

416
00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:01.300
That's all it is.

417
00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:01.660
Yeah.

418
00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:06.700
And it's usually Michael is the ridiculous one asking Pam or Jim or someone to do something,

419
00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:08.660
go to Hooters or whatever it was.

420
00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:14.740
It was either a sane person doing something sane and watching the lunatics respond or vice

421
00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:18.940
versa, like a lunatic doing something lunatic and then the sane person's responding.

422
00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.160
You also wrote Valentine's Day.

423
00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:22.780
Yeah.

424
00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:26.100
And this puts the character of Michael Scott in New York City.

425
00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:26.700
Yes.

426
00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:29.420
There are so many funny moments.

427
00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:31.400
What was it like to do that?

428
00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:32.980
Like you're from the East CoaSt.

429
00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:33.300
Yeah.

430
00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:36.900
So seeing New York City through Michael Scott's eyes.

431
00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:38.780
So that was season two.

432
00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:39.520
Season two.

433
00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:47.500
And it was the beginning of Greg wanting to expand the world of the show occasionally,

434
00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:54.340
rarely, but saying like we can break out a little bit of the rule we had stuck to,

435
00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:58.040
which is every episode happens in one day and every episode happens inside the walls of

436
00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:00.120
the office or on the grounds of Dunder Mifflin.

437
00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:02.760
And so we had this idea that he would go to New York.

438
00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.740
There were two really important things that I think came out of that episode.

439
00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:10.940
The first was like, let's take a skeleton crew to the city, walk around with Steve Carell

440
00:17:11.540 --> 00:17:13.140
and just do a bunch of jokes.

441
00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:15.940
In the room, I remember we called it Michael Scott's New York.

442
00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:20.660
That's how he thought of it was like, because his whole thing is I'm an expert on everything.

443
00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:21.080
Yes.

444
00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:25.280
And so we thought it would be really fun to put him in Manhattan, a place where like

445
00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:29.900
you've got to, you know, spend a lot of time in Manhattan to be an expert on it.

446
00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:30.900
And he is not.

447
00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:36.140
So the joke I wrote, that's probably the most famous joke of that episode is the one where

448
00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:39.240
he talks about taking the camera crew to his favorite pizza place.

449
00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:39.660
Yes.

450
00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:40.740
And it's a Sparrow.

451
00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:41.260
Yeah.

452
00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:43.780
It's the one that comes back to me the most still to this day.

453
00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:51.620
I mean, people post online pictures of themselves in front of Sparrow and saying, you know.

454
00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:51.920
Yeah.

455
00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:53.100
Got to get a New York slice.

456
00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:53.380
Yeah.

457
00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:53.720
Yeah.

458
00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:57.020
That episode is largely about him and Jan.

459
00:17:57.340 --> 00:17:57.620
Yes.

460
00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:02.340
And him believing that he has a relationship with Jan that he does not actually have.

461
00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:06.920
But this was the other really important thing about that episode from a like a world building

462
00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:13.660
point of view was a question that had been asked many times of the show by critics and

463
00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:18.360
fans and all sorts of people was, how did this dummy get this job?

464
00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:18.680
Right.

465
00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:20.200
How did Michael Scott get this job?

466
00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:22.120
And Greg had thought it out very carefully.

467
00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:26.420
It was a living example of what's called in corporate structure, the Peter Principle,

468
00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:26.620
right?

469
00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:26.960
Yes.

470
00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:28.440
We know the Peter Principle.

471
00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:29.640
We've discussed, right?

472
00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:33.920
People who do a good job at their job are then promoted to a position in which their skills

473
00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:36.700
have run out and are no longer applicable or not qualified.

474
00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:42.400
But it was still a question of like, okay, even given that this guy has done a lot of

475
00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:44.980
ridiculous stuff, so how do we justify it?

476
00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:49.700
So the whole point of this episode was we were like, we're going to see four guys, including

477
00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:52.420
Michael, who have Michael's job at different branches.

478
00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:58.360
Two of them are going to be demonstrably more competent than him, and one of them is going

479
00:18:58.360 --> 00:18:59.140
to be less competent.

480
00:18:59.420 --> 00:19:04.860
And like, we are going to absolutely show that there are people who have his job who are dumber

481
00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:05.360
than he is.

482
00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:09.780
And that was that character who just was like, I thought this was more of like a meet and

483
00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:10.340
greet kind of.

484
00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:12.540
Yeah, he's very funny, too.

485
00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:13.380
Yeah, he's very funny.

486
00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:14.920
Craig Anton, I think, is his name.

487
00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:21.120
And so Michael does something kind of important, too, in that episode, which is he has made

488
00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:22.900
a ridiculous video.

489
00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:29.660
The CFO, David Wallace, has asked him to make a presentation about his branch, numbers, facts,

490
00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:31.760
revenue streams, stuff like that.

491
00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:31.820
Sure, yeah.

492
00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:37.040
And instead, in classic Michael Scott fashion, he decides to make a video that's like a heart

493
00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:40.180
string tugging kind of like, these people matter to me.

494
00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:46.360
And he presents that video, and David Wallace's reaction is like, wow.

495
00:19:46.780 --> 00:19:49.240
But also, do you have the facts I asked for?

496
00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:51.360
And the key thing is that he does.

497
00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:52.720
That was like a big deal.

498
00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:56.160
He passes out these like really nice, like spiral bound.

499
00:19:56.160 --> 00:20:00.680
Yes, and it was a demonstration that like, even though he's an absurd person, he's not

500
00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:01.260
incompetent.

501
00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:02.480
He knows what he has to do.

502
00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:06.900
And he also, he was hoping that David Wallace would like start crying because he was so moved

503
00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:12.580
by his video that he would see what Michael sees in the incredible people of Dan Dunder,

504
00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:13.240
Mifflin, Scranton.

505
00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:15.400
And so that was the whole purpose of that.

506
00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:18.660
Besides, obviously, advancing the Michael Jan relationship was like-

507
00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:19.900
Because she gives him a kiss at the end.

508
00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:20.420
She does indeed.

509
00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:20.760
Yeah.

510
00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:26.600
She's like surprised by his maneuver that he like owns up to the relationship and takes

511
00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:28.340
the fall for it and protects her.

512
00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:34.140
And she's so, you know, sort of like pleased with how he handled it that she ends up giving

513
00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:38.360
him what he hoped he was going to get, which was like a romantic Valentine's Day, New

514
00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:39.040
York moment.

515
00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:42.720
There's another very important thing that came out of that episode.

516
00:20:43.240 --> 00:20:49.020
For me and for America, when we auditioned people for that role that Craig Anton did,

517
00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:49.920
and it was very funny.

518
00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:53.040
And one of the people we saw was Nick Offerman.

519
00:20:53.260 --> 00:20:53.680
Oh.

520
00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:56.080
And he auditioned for it.

521
00:20:56.180 --> 00:20:57.720
And we were like, that guy's really funny.

522
00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:02.360
But then it immediately came to us before we could, I think, even offer him the part,

523
00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:05.160
maybe, that he had a conflict.

524
00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:09.840
And the conflict was he was shooting an episode of Will and Grace at the same time, because

525
00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:11.780
he is famously married to Megan Mullally.

526
00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:18.220
So I wrote the name Nick Offerman on a post-it note and stuck it to my computer as a reminder

527
00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:20.860
to me that like this guy had auditioned and was really funny.

528
00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:26.320
And then three years later, when Greg and I were developing Parks and Rec, I was like,

529
00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:28.700
oh, that guy, that guy's really funny.

530
00:21:29.180 --> 00:21:33.840
And he came in and read for a bunch of parts and ended up playing Ron Swanson.

531
00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:37.760
And when the show ended, I saved the post-it note.

532
00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:38.400
Nice.

533
00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:39.800
And I had it framed.

534
00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:44.280
And it had, in the intervening time, it had like been, it had fallen off my computer and

535
00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:45.060
been stepped on.

536
00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:46.380
So there was like a footprint on it.

537
00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:49.600
And someone, some random phone number was also written on it or whatever.

538
00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:54.060
But I saved it and like put it in a frame and gave it to Nick as a present at the end

539
00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:54.720
of the show.

540
00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:56.740
But that was where, that was where I first saw him.

541
00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:03.400
Oh, I'm so glad you told that story for any like aspiring artists out there who have gone

542
00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:05.440
to an audition and then they don't get the part.

543
00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:10.760
But like, you might be on a post-it note on someone's computer, like that might grow

544
00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:12.760
into something in three years.

545
00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:13.240
Yeah.

546
00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:15.160
It was wonderful the way it all worked out.

547
00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:19.960
And it taught us a really important lesson, which was, and I think this started at the office.

548
00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:23.380
If it didn't, it definitely started at Parks and Rec or we took it to Parks and Rec.

549
00:22:23.380 --> 00:22:28.120
We used to have a folder on our computer that called good for something.

550
00:22:28.460 --> 00:22:30.880
And it would be like, we would audition people for roles.

551
00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:34.740
And then if we didn't cast them, but we liked them, we would just take their audition and

552
00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:35.620
throw it into that folder.

553
00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:40.480
And then in a future episode, if it was like, oh, who can play this part?

554
00:22:40.580 --> 00:22:43.640
We would go through that folder and look at all those auditions and we go, oh, that,

555
00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:44.740
that lady was funny.

556
00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:45.860
We'll get her to do it.

557
00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:50.420
And people would constantly on Parks and Rec show up and be like, I didn't audition for

558
00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:50.600
this.

559
00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:51.560
Like, how did this happen?

560
00:22:53.380 --> 00:22:56.440
Oh, and we found you and, you know, saved, we liked your audition and saved you.

561
00:22:56.760 --> 00:23:01.800
And it, and most of the actors always had that same reaction, which was like, thank

562
00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:02.500
you so much.

563
00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:02.940
Yes.

564
00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:04.960
But for me, it was like, well, why wouldn't you do that?

565
00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:06.320
It's like, you like an actor.

566
00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:07.680
They did a good job.

567
00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:11.440
Like actors don't grow on trees, like save them and use them in something, you know?

568
00:23:12.040 --> 00:23:12.660
Two things.

569
00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:15.980
One, I think it's very Michael Scott of you that you stuck it on your computer.

570
00:23:16.780 --> 00:23:18.100
It is very old timey.

571
00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:24.140
I usually, I did tidy up, but I normally have post-it notes and I opened my laptop in there

572
00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:24.480
here.

573
00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:26.760
I don't want to like forget.

574
00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:32.420
But then also you said, you know, actors don't grow on trees, which made me think of

575
00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:33.780
branch closing.

576
00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:35.920
Cause I watched it this morning and I love it.

577
00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:36.680
Oh my gosh.

578
00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:37.140
Yes.

579
00:23:37.220 --> 00:23:39.180
It's always been one of my favorite episodes.

580
00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:42.040
And I was like, I haven't watched it in a bit since we like first broke it down.

581
00:23:42.040 --> 00:23:45.580
But did you write Stanley doesn't grow on trees?

582
00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:46.660
I believe I did.

583
00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:46.860
Yeah.

584
00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:47.960
Because you just said it.

585
00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:50.660
So it might be just deep within you.

586
00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:52.480
Show me that farm where, yeah.

587
00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:52.760
Yeah.

588
00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:54.160
Where Stanleys and Phyllises.

589
00:23:54.340 --> 00:23:55.820
We just Stanleys and Phyllises grow on trees.

590
00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:57.920
It's such a good episode.

591
00:23:58.100 --> 00:23:59.560
I was, it like made my morning.

592
00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:01.000
I like watched it with a cup of tea.

593
00:24:01.120 --> 00:24:04.620
I remember thinking like that's season three.

594
00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:05.300
Yes.

595
00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:06.120
It's season three.

596
00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:11.860
And I remember thinking, I remember watching that episode, but watching Steve specifically

597
00:24:11.860 --> 00:24:15.700
in that episode and thinking like, this is an actor at the height of his powers.

598
00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:19.400
Like it's a great showcase for him for a couple of reasons.

599
00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:23.800
One is the very first scene is Jan coming in and saying, this branch is closing.

600
00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:26.500
And he just is usually on the show.

601
00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:31.740
Like there's a little bit of a ramp in, you know, like starts with some slice of life.

602
00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:33.400
And then Michael has some dumb thing.

603
00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:37.100
And then whatever, eventually like a couple minutes in the premise has sort of sprung.

604
00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:42.620
That episode, Jan walks in and he's like going, Jan Levinson Gould, blah, blah, blah, blah.

605
00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:46.840
And then she just goes, just call me Levinson in the morning.

606
00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:50.820
And then she's like, I'm here to tell you the branch is closing.

607
00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:55.180
And so 30 seconds in, he gets the worst news of his life.

608
00:24:55.180 --> 00:25:02.380
And that monologue, that talking head he gives of like, show me that farm is so good.

609
00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:08.620
And it's like, he has so deeply internalized the degree to which this office is his entire life.

610
00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:10.980
It's like an incredible performance.

611
00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:17.620
And I remember that was the year, not to divert the conversation too much into something that doesn't really matter.

612
00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:24.140
But that was the year that everyone was like, Steve Carell is going to win the Emmy for best actor in a comedy.

613
00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:25.480
It is a foregone conclusion.

614
00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:25.820
In fact,

615
00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:37.920
it was such a foregone conclusion that that year they had Stephen Colbert and Jon Stewart present the category because it was going to be a daily show correspondence reunion on stage.

616
00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:40.220
And they were going to give it to their friend, Steve Carell.

617
00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:49.460
And then I believe Ricky Gervais ended up winning for the show Extras, which was a BBC show that HBO had just bought and like thrown on the air.

618
00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:50.560
And he wasn't even there.

619
00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:53.880
It was one of those deals where they showed a black and white photo of him on the screen.

620
00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:56.620
And they were like, Ricky Gervais isn't here.

621
00:25:56.740 --> 00:25:59.280
So instead, we're going to give this to our friend Steve Carell.

622
00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:02.380
And then Steve jumped up and ran on stage and like bowed and everyone cheered.

623
00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:09.640
But I remember thinking of all of the robberies that occurred from folks who worked on The Office over the years,

624
00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:13.600
that season three best actor in a comedy was number one on my liSt.

625
00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:20.060
Because he was the funniest person in America and he was the best actor on a comedy in America by a wide margin.

626
00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:24.820
And I always think of that performance in that episode as exhibit A in that argument.

627
00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:25.320
Yeah.

628
00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:26.820
He's fantastic in it.

629
00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:27.040
Yeah.

630
00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:29.300
And he never won an Emmy for the role of Michael Scott.

631
00:26:29.300 --> 00:26:29.660
He never did.

632
00:26:29.660 --> 00:26:30.280
He never did.

633
00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:37.940
When I speak to acting classes, I remind them of that fact that you can do the best work.

634
00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:40.660
You can be the very, very best at what you do.

635
00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:41.100
Yeah.

636
00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:41.640
I know.

637
00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:48.980
At this point, it's like you can make a better cast and show out of the folks who have never won than the folks who have won, I dare say.

638
00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:50.840
And he's, you know, he's the lead.

639
00:26:51.160 --> 00:26:51.400
Yeah.

640
00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:53.340
They had a camera on us.

641
00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:54.680
I've told this story before.

642
00:26:55.240 --> 00:26:56.340
We were in a row together.

643
00:26:56.560 --> 00:26:59.880
It was like Jenna and John and me in the same row.

644
00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:01.500
And Steve was in front of us.

645
00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:06.280
And, you know, when they're announcing the nominees, the camera guys just run right up.

646
00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:06.530
Right.

647
00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:07.540
And they just put a camera on.

648
00:27:07.660 --> 00:27:07.840
Right in your face.

649
00:27:07.940 --> 00:27:08.620
Right in your face.

650
00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:10.260
And so it's right in Steve's face.

651
00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:12.140
And then we're directly behind him.

652
00:27:12.440 --> 00:27:15.920
And when they didn't say his name, I guess I said, mother f***er.

653
00:27:18.500 --> 00:27:20.220
I mean, that's the correct response.

654
00:27:20.580 --> 00:27:20.780
Yeah.

655
00:27:21.180 --> 00:27:24.680
And I had one or two friends say, did you say mother f***er?

656
00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:26.400
I think I saw you say that.

657
00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:27.520
And I'm like, yes.

658
00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:28.180
Yes, I did.

659
00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:28.980
It came out of me.

660
00:27:29.060 --> 00:27:30.080
I didn't have a choice.

661
00:27:30.460 --> 00:27:32.060
I couldn't believe it.

662
00:27:32.220 --> 00:27:32.580
I couldn't.

663
00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:35.120
And I, you know, so many of us couldn't because he was amazing.

664
00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:40.020
The other scene in that episode that I think of when I think of how great he was is the one where,

665
00:27:40.160 --> 00:27:44.800
so the plot is that he and Dwight decide that they're going to go to David Wallace's house.

666
00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:45.120
Yes.

667
00:27:45.340 --> 00:27:48.920
And just like stop him and like make their argument why Scranton shouldn't close.

668
00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:50.440
And he never shows up.

669
00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:52.820
They just end up spending the entire day outside his house.

670
00:27:53.980 --> 00:27:57.760
And he and Dwight are sitting there at the end when it looks like all is loSt.

671
00:27:57.760 --> 00:28:04.920
And he just says like, all right, top three, what are your top three moments ever, your three favorite moments at Dunder Mifflin?

672
00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:12.880
And Dwight names a couple, one or two things I think we've seen in the world of the show, like the concussion, like when Michael took Dwight.

673
00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:13.760
It took him to the hospital.

674
00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:15.420
And Dwight gets a little emotional.

675
00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:15.980
Gets a little emotional.

676
00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:21.840
And there's also one thing I think that we didn't see that was a reference to something that happened before the documentary started shooting.

677
00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:25.340
And then Dwight says, what about you?

678
00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:26.380
What are your top three moments?

679
00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:31.360
And he says, all of them, every single moment, every single moment was wonderful and perfect and special.

680
00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:33.580
And it's really a beautiful moment.

681
00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:37.480
And then Dwight says, what about when Jan came and told you the office was closing?

682
00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:38.660
And he's like, God damn it, Dwight.

683
00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:41.060
It was so perfect.

684
00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:43.760
Dwight, like who he is.

685
00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:44.020
Yeah.

686
00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:44.920
He's so literal.

687
00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:47.580
He's extremely, every single moment.

688
00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:48.760
Well, I can think of one.

689
00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:49.100
Yeah.

690
00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:58.880
He also, they did this great thing where Dwight is going to practice being David Wallace and Michael's going to plea his case, why they can't close Scranton Branch.

691
00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:04.800
And Dwight's just very matter of factly is like, well, it's a dying business for one.

692
00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:07.820
And it's like, it just comes down to numbers.

693
00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:08.760
It's just math.

694
00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:10.780
And he's so matter of fact.

695
00:29:11.080 --> 00:29:12.380
And Michael's like, stop it, stop it.

696
00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:13.640
He's never going to say that.

697
00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:16.900
Well, there's a wonderful moment that was improvised between the two of them.

698
00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:21.440
I think the way it ended was Michael was like, just start over and be more scared of me.

699
00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:26.200
And so Dwight goes back and he wasn't supposed to do this, but Rain walked up again.

700
00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:28.520
And Michael's was like, David Wallace.

701
00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:29.460
And Rain goes, ha!

702
00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:31.400
And jumps backwards.

703
00:29:32.780 --> 00:29:34.200
And we had to cut it off.

704
00:29:34.400 --> 00:29:38.980
Literally the frame where we cut it off is the last frame before they both started laughing.

705
00:29:39.300 --> 00:29:41.640
It's a good memory of that moment.

706
00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:43.940
Well, it was really fun to re-watch it.

707
00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:45.300
I highly recommend, guys.

708
00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:49.060
Well, since we're talking about season three, this is actually a question I wanted to ask you.

709
00:29:49.480 --> 00:29:52.840
The arc of season three is such an impressive arc.

710
00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:55.360
You have the whole storyline of Jim going to Stanford.

711
00:29:55.380 --> 00:29:57.300
You had to create this whole other branch.

712
00:29:57.440 --> 00:30:03.400
You had to fill it with people who were then going to come merge with the Scranton branch.

713
00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:05.740
And then some of them stay.

714
00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:07.520
Like you had to cast Rashida Jones.

715
00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:09.140
You had to cast Ed HelMs.

716
00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:12.360
It was this massive ongoing storyline.

717
00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:15.660
Like, can you talk a little bit about how that was created?

718
00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:20.940
Like some of the conversations in the room and like how far into the season did you get when we started shooting?

719
00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:22.100
It's a great question.

720
00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:28.000
This was a big swing that Greg decided to make, which was at the end of season two after Jim and Pam kiss.

721
00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:33.740
And Pam sort of lets him down in her mind easy, but for him not very easy.

722
00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:35.460
Greg was like, he's got to leave.

723
00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:39.740
We can't just start season three with him like back at the office.

724
00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:43.140
It's not sort of following through on the story we've told.

725
00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:45.820
The story we've told is that Jim stays in this office because of Pam.

726
00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:49.500
And so if he makes a play for Pam and Pam turns him down, he's going to leave.

727
00:30:50.220 --> 00:30:52.820
So he had a general with Ed, I remember.

728
00:30:53.140 --> 00:30:55.280
Like, and we were all Daily Show fans.

729
00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:56.760
And so we were like, oh, Ed Helms is here.

730
00:30:56.820 --> 00:30:57.180
That's fun.

731
00:30:58.180 --> 00:31:02.460
And we came into that season and Greg was like, Jim is going to leave.

732
00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:04.160
He's going to go to another branch.

733
00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:07.720
We're going to meet an entire group of people who work in that branch.

734
00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:11.100
And then at some point early in the season, they're going to fold together.

735
00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:12.160
The branches are going to merge.

736
00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:19.360
So the important people were Ed's character, Andy Bernard, Rashida's character, who was going to be Jim's new love intereSt.

737
00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:22.020
Because Greg was like, when Jim comes back, he's got a girlfriend.

738
00:31:22.380 --> 00:31:23.760
That was a huge thing too.

739
00:31:24.400 --> 00:31:34.000
And then the manager of the office, who was Josh, who was going to play an important role in the story in that he was going to seem like an incredibly competent,

740
00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:40.840
like well-functioning, high-functioning manager who is clearly better than Michael Scott at managing,

741
00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:50.140
but was also like kind of a self-interested guy who didn't really care about the office and was going to leverage this corporate upheaval for his own gain.

742
00:31:50.140 --> 00:31:56.320
Which led to one of my favorite Jim lines, where when that is revealed, when Jim says like, say what you will about Michael Scott, he would never do that.

743
00:31:56.740 --> 00:32:00.080
Which was like, that was the whole storyline that was laid out.

744
00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:01.360
And Charles Esten was great.

745
00:32:01.460 --> 00:32:02.300
He was so good.

746
00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:02.620
Yeah.

747
00:32:02.820 --> 00:32:03.640
He was really great.

748
00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:09.860
He was a perfect, just like handsome, charismatic guy who like everyone was like psyched to work for.

749
00:32:09.860 --> 00:32:14.100
And, you know, the hardest part, obviously, like Ed, you cast because Ed's hilarious.

750
00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:20.560
And like, he is like a guy who you're like, well, this guy will fold naturally into any ensemble and will be wonderful and great.

751
00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:22.960
Can I say one thing about that episode?

752
00:32:23.260 --> 00:32:33.220
When they find out in branch closing that, you know, Scranton is going to be, you know, closed down, he goes up to Jim at the Stanford office and goes, in your face!

753
00:32:34.280 --> 00:32:36.520
Like, Jim's like, I don't work there.

754
00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:38.020
I work here.

755
00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:44.660
Of all of Krasinski's tiny line readings, it's one of the tiniest because he goes, hi, in your face.

756
00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:46.960
And John goes, well, I work here now.

757
00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:47.280
Yeah.

758
00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:49.620
You can barely hear him.

759
00:32:49.660 --> 00:32:50.460
He speaks so quietly.

760
00:32:50.640 --> 00:32:51.000
It's wonderful.

761
00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:52.300
But anyway, I'm so sorry.

762
00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:53.960
So, so Ed, obviously.

763
00:32:54.140 --> 00:32:55.460
Ed is like, cast that guy.

764
00:32:55.600 --> 00:32:56.040
He's great.

765
00:32:56.280 --> 00:32:57.340
He's a New England preppy.

766
00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:01.920
Like, I was delighted because Andy Brown is everyone I grew up with in Connecticut.

767
00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:04.560
Like, I was like, oh, I can write this guy forever.

768
00:33:04.840 --> 00:33:08.340
Like, I'm giving, like, wind me up and set me loose.

769
00:33:08.720 --> 00:33:18.140
The hardest part was Rashida because this is one of the hardest things to do in any show, right, is there are two people on the show who are soulmates.

770
00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:20.320
It's very clear that they're soulmates.

771
00:33:20.460 --> 00:33:22.860
And they are going to, at some point, get together.

772
00:33:23.020 --> 00:33:25.300
And Greg was like, if they get together, they're staying together.

773
00:33:25.300 --> 00:33:33.560
We're not doing Ross and Rachel, which they did, obviously, incredibly effectively over 10 years, which is a get together, break up, get together, break up over and over again.

774
00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:36.100
He's like, when Jim and Pam get together, they're going to stay together.

775
00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:37.300
He always kind of knew that.

776
00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:46.660
So, what we needed for the obstacle was a woman who Jim could date, who was a good match for him.

777
00:33:46.880 --> 00:33:52.660
He also didn't want to do a move, which I think is kind of crappy and slightly misogynistic,

778
00:33:52.680 --> 00:33:57.460
which is you give the protagonist of the show a girlfriend who's just a terrible person.

779
00:33:57.460 --> 00:34:05.160
He either cheats on him or browbeats him or is, like, selfish or is mean or whatever, and it's cheap because it makes the audience just, like,

780
00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:08.320
root for Jim and for the person to go with you or the other person.

781
00:34:08.600 --> 00:34:10.080
And Greg was like, that's just fake.

782
00:34:10.160 --> 00:34:11.440
Like, you know that's fake.

783
00:34:11.440 --> 00:34:16.340
And Greg, he had this very, very Greg-like analogy, which is he told the story.

784
00:34:16.440 --> 00:34:21.000
I want to say it's General Sherman in the Civil War.

785
00:34:21.220 --> 00:34:21.620
Oh, my gosh.

786
00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:23.100
It's a classic Greg thing.

787
00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:23.700
Amazing.

788
00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:24.160
Yeah.

789
00:34:24.160 --> 00:34:27.220
Did he tell it while laying on his back, stretching his hamstrings?

790
00:34:27.220 --> 00:34:28.620
A hundred percent he did, yes.

791
00:34:28.740 --> 00:34:29.080
His hamstrings.

792
00:34:29.300 --> 00:34:31.920
He was lying on the floor, on the dirty floor of the right of his room.

793
00:34:32.220 --> 00:34:35.980
The story from history was that I think it was Sherman.

794
00:34:35.980 --> 00:34:36.880
It might have been Grant.

795
00:34:37.720 --> 00:34:39.380
Or I'm getting all of this wrong.

796
00:34:39.540 --> 00:34:43.400
But it was an army was marching south through the U.S.

797
00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:49.360
And the opposing forces, the Confederate forces, were very skilled fighters.

798
00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:52.860
And even if they were outnumbered, they could win a theoretical battle.

799
00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:54.340
If they knew where they were going.

800
00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:58.880
So if Sherman were marching on Richmond, Lee would marshal all of his forces in Richmond

801
00:34:58.880 --> 00:34:59.660
and be ready for him.

802
00:34:59.840 --> 00:35:06.500
So what Sherman did, according to Greg, was to drive his army halfway between two possible

803
00:35:06.500 --> 00:35:09.320
destinations, halfway between Richmond and Atlanta.

804
00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:14.600
So Lee would then have to send half of his army to Richmond and half to Atlanta.

805
00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:18.400
And then at the last second, Sherman would break towards one of them.

806
00:35:18.400 --> 00:35:22.220
And then he would only have to fight half of the Southern army and would win.

807
00:35:22.720 --> 00:35:27.840
And he used this analogy by saying, like, we have to create a girlfriend for Jim who

808
00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:32.920
is compelling enough and a good enough match that half of our audience is rooting for him

809
00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:35.440
to say, I'm sorry, Pam, you missed your shot.

810
00:35:35.620 --> 00:35:39.380
And half of the audience is rooting for him to say, like, get rid of this lady and go with

811
00:35:39.380 --> 00:35:39.700
Pam.

812
00:35:39.700 --> 00:35:42.720
And so that required a lot of things.

813
00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:49.100
One, some good stories where Rashida was funny and interesting and compelling and smart and

814
00:35:49.100 --> 00:35:49.480
cool.

815
00:35:49.900 --> 00:35:54.260
And it required an actor to play that role that people wouldn't hate.

816
00:35:54.480 --> 00:35:56.400
And so we looked forever.

817
00:35:56.400 --> 00:36:03.000
We tried so many different versions of like just combing through the other shows and seeing

818
00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:06.420
like what actors have been on these shows who seem good and funny and whatever.

819
00:36:06.420 --> 00:36:10.300
I met Rashida my second day of college and we've been friends ever since.

820
00:36:10.480 --> 00:36:14.320
And so I was obviously like quietly being like, cast Rashida.

821
00:36:14.620 --> 00:36:14.960
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

822
00:36:15.160 --> 00:36:16.660
And then she had met John already.

823
00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:18.920
And so John liked her and was like pushing for her.

824
00:36:19.140 --> 00:36:26.340
But Greg famously indecisive in these moments, or I guess the indecisive is not the nicest

825
00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:26.900
way to say it.

826
00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:31.740
Thorough would be the nicest way to put it, that he exhausts every avenue of possibility.

827
00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:35.080
And we looked and looked and looked and eventually settled on Rashida.

828
00:36:35.540 --> 00:36:41.460
And then the challenge became in like six episodes, introduce her and build her into a

829
00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:46.020
person who at least some of the audience was like, wait, I think she's a better girlfriend

830
00:36:46.020 --> 00:36:47.080
for Jim than Pam is.

831
00:36:47.300 --> 00:36:48.860
And that was really hard.

832
00:36:48.860 --> 00:36:53.960
And that took like most of the time of the writer's room and the time between season two

833
00:36:53.960 --> 00:36:55.740
and season three was spent just doing that.

834
00:36:55.880 --> 00:37:00.600
Like how can we like meticulously form a character that audiences are rooting for?

835
00:37:00.600 --> 00:37:01.540
It was very hard.

836
00:37:01.780 --> 00:37:07.440
You know what I think worked about it is that I liked Karen as a woman.

837
00:37:07.740 --> 00:37:09.600
Like I wanted to go to lunch with Karen.

838
00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:11.060
I wanted to be friends with Karen.

839
00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:13.660
I'm like, oh, this is a woman I'd want to spend time with.

840
00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:14.940
She's cool.

841
00:37:15.140 --> 00:37:15.320
Yeah.

842
00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:18.920
And they seemed like exactly what you're saying.

843
00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:24.960
Like Jim and Karen seemed like a couple who were compatible enough and had enough fun

844
00:37:24.960 --> 00:37:28.060
that they'd have like a three-year relationship and then break up.

845
00:37:28.180 --> 00:37:28.320
Right.

846
00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:35.000
You know, like Pam aside, Jim was not going to end up with Karen in the long run.

847
00:37:35.140 --> 00:37:35.220
Right.

848
00:37:35.540 --> 00:37:36.600
Whenever he met her.

849
00:37:36.840 --> 00:37:36.920
Right.

850
00:37:37.200 --> 00:37:39.200
But this was like a solid choice.

851
00:37:39.420 --> 00:37:42.000
This was like a person to spend three years of your time with.

852
00:37:42.140 --> 00:37:42.360
Yeah.

853
00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:43.120
She was cool.

854
00:37:43.400 --> 00:37:44.100
She was smart.

855
00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:45.440
She was good at her job.

856
00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:46.200
She was funny.

857
00:37:46.520 --> 00:37:46.700
Yeah.

858
00:37:46.840 --> 00:37:53.920
A huge breakthrough for the writers was the Christmas episode that year, which was extended,

859
00:37:53.920 --> 00:37:57.740
which Jen Salata wrote, I want to say, and which was so good.

860
00:37:57.920 --> 00:38:00.040
And Harold Ramis, the late Harold Ramis directed.

861
00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:00.800
Yes.

862
00:38:00.800 --> 00:38:06.540
And was so good that it was the first time that the cut came in at like 42 minutes, as

863
00:38:06.540 --> 00:38:07.100
it always did.

864
00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:12.640
And instead of cutting it down to 21, 30, Greg went to the studio and was like, we should

865
00:38:12.640 --> 00:38:13.740
make this a two-parter.

866
00:38:13.820 --> 00:38:14.700
We should make this an hour.

867
00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:19.680
And we ended up shooting another like writing and shooting another like six or eight minutes

868
00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:22.200
of stuff in order to then boil it down to the best 43.

869
00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:28.780
But the plot that mattered so much was that Karen and Pam were on the same team.

870
00:38:29.080 --> 00:38:29.240
Yeah.

871
00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:29.900
Yeah.

872
00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:32.420
It was like Churchill, right?

873
00:38:32.560 --> 00:38:34.760
Like nothing brings people together like a common enemy.

874
00:38:35.140 --> 00:38:35.240
Yes.

875
00:38:35.380 --> 00:38:36.280
And so there you were.

876
00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:36.580
Yeah.

877
00:38:37.980 --> 00:38:45.380
And like that, but that pitching out that story was a huge relief for us because we were

878
00:38:45.380 --> 00:38:50.980
like, right, the story of this doesn't have to be a rivalry between Pam and Karen.

879
00:38:50.980 --> 00:38:56.020
It can be that, like you said, that like Pam is like, I kind of like this lady.

880
00:38:56.200 --> 00:39:01.240
Like I want to be friends with her, like legitimately, not as a way to like weasel my way back into

881
00:39:01.240 --> 00:39:06.700
Jim's life, but just because for Pam, life in that office could be pretty lonely in terms

882
00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:09.080
of like ladies that you can hang out with and talk to.

883
00:39:09.280 --> 00:39:11.900
And suddenly here's one right here and she's cool.

884
00:39:12.040 --> 00:39:17.480
And so in the like slow, deliberate sort of brick by brick construction of Karen as a

885
00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:21.500
character, that was like a huge moment that we were like, oh, there should be an episode

886
00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:25.040
where they happily and joyfully find themselves on the same team.

887
00:39:25.440 --> 00:39:32.500
I think shout out to, to Rashida, because she just has a natural presence as Karen that

888
00:39:32.500 --> 00:39:33.540
puts you at ease.

889
00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:33.840
Yeah.

890
00:39:34.220 --> 00:39:37.320
And that's her just instincts as an actor.

891
00:39:37.380 --> 00:39:40.620
And then also in real life, she is one of the coolest people you'll ever meet.

892
00:39:40.620 --> 00:39:44.020
She is like, you know, I took a trip with her.

893
00:39:44.220 --> 00:39:45.520
Like we've talked about this before.

894
00:39:45.700 --> 00:39:50.140
I went and did this work with this nonprofit that helps save the world's oceans.

895
00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:55.560
And Rashida and I went together and there are still things she said as we traveled to

896
00:39:55.560 --> 00:40:00.980
Belize, like that I still say to people like, like she emotionally expensive.

897
00:40:01.300 --> 00:40:02.100
That's one of them.

898
00:40:02.200 --> 00:40:05.680
She said, I kind of was like going through some stuff and she was like, Angela, that person

899
00:40:05.680 --> 00:40:07.240
is too emotionally expensive for you.

900
00:40:07.340 --> 00:40:08.340
And I was like, what?

901
00:40:08.680 --> 00:40:09.540
This is brilliant.

902
00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:11.640
The other thing is she gets on a plane.

903
00:40:11.840 --> 00:40:12.840
She's like a professional.

904
00:40:13.020 --> 00:40:18.940
She has so many lotions and she hydrates and she's like, my friends call me lotions and

905
00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:19.960
potions on planes.

906
00:40:21.060 --> 00:40:26.060
Now I like bring like hand lotion and chapstick like on a plane.

907
00:40:26.200 --> 00:40:26.920
And I think of her.

908
00:40:27.100 --> 00:40:32.420
She was, I mean, again, I'm coming from a medium sized town in suburban Connecticut.

909
00:40:32.780 --> 00:40:39.120
But even so, when I met her and on the second day of freshman year of college in 1993,

910
00:40:39.120 --> 00:40:41.040
she was the coolest person I'd ever met.

911
00:40:41.680 --> 00:40:46.280
And now 33 years later, she is still the coolest person I've ever met.

912
00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:51.360
Well, I think to wrap up this part of our office convo, we wanted to ask you, and this

913
00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:54.440
is like kind of one of those questions, like when people ask me, I'm like, oh, it's so hard.

914
00:40:54.580 --> 00:40:58.680
But do you have an episode that you wrote that's near and dear to your heart?

915
00:40:59.380 --> 00:41:01.220
I mean, all of them, obviously.

916
00:41:01.580 --> 00:41:02.860
I will say branch closing.

917
00:41:03.380 --> 00:41:07.220
I was very delighted in how it all turned out.

918
00:41:07.420 --> 00:41:13.720
I think the one that I think of the most is the negotiation, which was one of the super

919
00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:17.920
sizey ones, which I was so happy about because there was so much going on.

920
00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:26.080
It's the one that starts with Dwight spraying mace at Pam's boyfriend and stopping an attack.

921
00:41:26.080 --> 00:41:30.300
And Angela's titillated by it and keeps having everyone retell the story.

922
00:41:30.300 --> 00:41:35.200
So this was the best part of it was Roy comes in and Dwight maces him.

923
00:41:35.320 --> 00:41:38.200
He's about to attack Jim and maces him.

924
00:41:38.280 --> 00:41:41.560
And that's a great beginning to an episode, right?

925
00:41:41.620 --> 00:41:42.340
That's so exciting.

926
00:41:42.940 --> 00:41:46.500
And then a bunch of stuff has to branch out of that.

927
00:41:46.600 --> 00:41:51.400
And because the story that we had for Michael was that Daryl wanted to renegotiate his salary

928
00:41:51.400 --> 00:41:55.800
because Roy had been fired and now he was like, okay, I'm stepping up and whatever.

929
00:41:56.360 --> 00:41:59.340
And so that whole thing, that whole story is huge.

930
00:41:59.500 --> 00:42:04.720
And they go to the corporate offices and he talks to Jan and it also flips and Daryl finds

931
00:42:04.720 --> 00:42:07.560
out how much Michael makes and is like humiliated for him.

932
00:42:07.680 --> 00:42:11.480
And it's like, you got to get a, like, you have to get a raise when I can get a raise,

933
00:42:11.540 --> 00:42:11.740
you know?

934
00:42:12.160 --> 00:42:15.180
And it's a real Craig Robinson showcase that whole episode.

935
00:42:15.180 --> 00:42:22.820
But then the B story was Jim feeling like he owed Dwight a debt of gratitude and was

936
00:42:22.820 --> 00:42:24.180
like, how do I pay this guy back?

937
00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:26.360
And he tries all these silly ways to pay Dwight back.

938
00:42:26.520 --> 00:42:32.620
And Dwight in his like weird code of honor is refusing to accept a payback because he says

939
00:42:32.620 --> 00:42:36.040
citizens do not accept prizes for being citizens is that way he puts it.

940
00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:39.180
So both of those stories were so great.

941
00:42:39.340 --> 00:42:43.500
It was like, put Michael Scott and Craig Robinson in a room together and like send them off

942
00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:44.480
and it's going to be great.

943
00:42:44.480 --> 00:42:49.820
And also Jim feeling a debt to Dwight that he's trying desperately to fill because he hates

944
00:42:49.820 --> 00:42:51.980
the feeling of owing Dwight something fantastic.

945
00:42:52.500 --> 00:42:57.380
The problem was the best episodes of the show were always ones where everyone was involved.

946
00:42:57.600 --> 00:43:02.480
And you're talking about two huge stories where it's just Michael and Craig, really Toby

947
00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:04.480
at the end, and then Dwight and Jim.

948
00:43:04.880 --> 00:43:10.000
And so this was a risky maneuver, but on my own, when I went off to write it, I was like,

949
00:43:10.120 --> 00:43:11.360
I'm going to add another story.

950
00:43:11.360 --> 00:43:19.760
And the story was that Angela heard the tale of heroism for Dwight and was like turned on

951
00:43:19.760 --> 00:43:24.520
by it and kept asking other people to like, well, I didn't really hear what happened.

952
00:43:24.680 --> 00:43:25.660
Can you repeat the story?

953
00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:29.200
And she's doing like, like fingering, playing her necklace.

954
00:43:30.340 --> 00:43:36.740
And then the wonderful thing that came out of it was that Jim had hit a wall where he just

955
00:43:36.740 --> 00:43:38.960
couldn't figure out how to pay Dwight back.

956
00:43:39.100 --> 00:43:44.640
And he came out of the bathroom at the end and saw Dwight because Angela has gone up to

957
00:43:44.640 --> 00:43:49.340
Dwight and been like, I've heard of some, some incredible tales of heroism and there's no one

958
00:43:49.340 --> 00:43:54.720
in the office, they think, and they start making out and Jim sees it and he gets so excited.

959
00:43:54.720 --> 00:44:00.600
And he's like, I, and then he realizes and says, I'm never going to say a word.

960
00:44:00.620 --> 00:44:02.640
And that is how I will pay Dwight back.

961
00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:07.680
And it was like the perfect, like dovetailing of stories that involve like everyone in the

962
00:44:07.680 --> 00:44:08.980
office entirely.

963
00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:15.300
And so the way that all kind of coalesced into a big, giant, fun story.

964
00:44:15.300 --> 00:44:21.740
And then we had this wonderful thing for like another bunch of episodes, which was that Dwight

965
00:44:21.740 --> 00:44:26.960
thought that no one knew and Jim, his worst enemy knew this thing and wasn't telling anyone.

966
00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:29.140
And I think it just made Jim really likable.

967
00:44:29.380 --> 00:44:32.840
And he was like doing a nice thing without Dwight knowing it, which was just like a really

968
00:44:32.840 --> 00:44:34.420
sweet little backstory, you know?

969
00:44:35.000 --> 00:44:35.780
Oh, yeah.

970
00:44:36.080 --> 00:44:36.960
I love that.

971
00:44:37.100 --> 00:44:37.280
Yeah.

972
00:44:37.380 --> 00:44:38.460
That one was really fun.

973
00:44:38.460 --> 00:44:42.500
And I was so happy that it was supersized because it allowed us to keep all that stuff in the

974
00:44:42.500 --> 00:44:42.900
episode.

975
00:44:43.140 --> 00:44:43.360
Yeah.

976
00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:45.080
Oh my gosh.

