1206
00:56:56.740 --> 00:57:02.400
nails and like fall into deep spells of depression when their team loses and celebrate euphorically

1207
00:57:02.400 --> 00:57:03.020
when they win?

1208
00:57:03.560 --> 00:57:07.060
And I just thought that I was like, this is a phenomenon that's worth investigating.

1209
00:57:07.300 --> 00:57:11.540
So I called him and pitched him the idea and he was like, immediately saw it and we were

1210
00:57:11.540 --> 00:57:12.080
off and running.

1211
00:57:12.240 --> 00:57:15.960
And so we spent like a year and a half going to a bunch of different events.

1212
00:57:16.060 --> 00:57:18.560
We do go to like mainstream American sports.

1213
00:57:18.560 --> 00:57:20.880
We go to basketball, football, baseball.

1214
00:57:21.240 --> 00:57:23.480
I went to the Olympics in Paris and watched the U.S.

1215
00:57:23.680 --> 00:57:24.580
men's basketball team.

1216
00:57:24.740 --> 00:57:28.100
He went to a Montreal Canadiens hockey game with some friends in Montreal.

1217
00:57:28.100 --> 00:57:32.820
Like we do the stuff that matters most to Americans, but we also went to some really fun

1218
00:57:32.820 --> 00:57:34.320
stuff that we had never heard of before.

1219
00:57:34.640 --> 00:57:39.460
Joe went to the Indigenous Stickball Championships in Philadelphia, Mississippi, which is a sport

1220
00:57:39.460 --> 00:57:44.980
that native tribes have used to like settle disputes for like hundreds and hundreds and

1221
00:57:44.980 --> 00:57:45.780
hundreds of years.

1222
00:57:46.000 --> 00:57:51.340
It's sort of like field hockey or lacrosse, but it's like played by like dozens and dozens

1223
00:57:51.340 --> 00:57:52.440
of people at the same time.

1224
00:57:52.440 --> 00:57:59.140
It's sort of like lacrosse plus rugby plus like a fistfight and it's, you know, it's

1225
00:57:59.140 --> 00:58:04.060
in the middle of Mississippi in the summer and it's 140 degrees with 140% humidity.

1226
00:58:04.320 --> 00:58:10.440
Like we went to places where there are games that matter and we tried to like figure out

1227
00:58:10.440 --> 00:58:13.980
what it was about that unites all of them and the fans who love them.

1228
00:58:14.200 --> 00:58:19.060
But you also went to like a Taylor Swift concert, which is another kind of fandom.

1229
00:58:19.060 --> 00:58:21.840
Yeah, so we didn't want it to just be about sports.

1230
00:58:22.080 --> 00:58:28.100
So one of the things that we did was there were two really fun things that were non-sports

1231
00:58:28.100 --> 00:58:28.320
related.

1232
00:58:28.480 --> 00:58:33.140
One of them was I had the idea that we should each send the other one to something that the

1233
00:58:33.140 --> 00:58:33.840
other one hated.

1234
00:58:34.240 --> 00:58:34.600
Okay.

1235
00:58:34.800 --> 00:58:38.040
It was like, I know you hate this now, but go see if you can figure out why people like

1236
00:58:38.040 --> 00:58:38.220
it.

1237
00:58:38.380 --> 00:58:42.960
So I sent Joe to play pickleball in a pickleball tournament because he's a tennis player and

1238
00:58:42.960 --> 00:58:44.280
he's like, yeah, there you go.

1239
00:58:44.360 --> 00:58:44.700
I know.

1240
00:58:44.880 --> 00:58:46.680
It's like pickleball's taking over and I hate it.

1241
00:58:46.760 --> 00:58:47.400
I hate the sound.

1242
00:58:47.400 --> 00:58:49.000
I hate the kitchen.

1243
00:58:49.360 --> 00:58:50.880
I hate that it's named after the guy's dog.

1244
00:58:51.120 --> 00:58:52.200
Like he just hates everything about it.

1245
00:58:52.800 --> 00:58:57.620
But he sent me to WrestleMania because I don't care at all about wrestling.

1246
00:58:57.920 --> 00:58:59.320
I don't like combat sports.

1247
00:58:59.320 --> 00:59:00.520
I think they're ridiculous.

1248
00:59:01.500 --> 00:59:02.700
And so I was like, all right, fine.

1249
00:59:02.700 --> 00:59:03.420
I'll go to WrestleMania.

1250
00:59:03.760 --> 00:59:07.500
And then my agent called me and was like, hey, I'm taking a bunch of people to a dead

1251
00:59:07.500 --> 00:59:09.740
in company show in Vegas for my birthday.

1252
00:59:10.420 --> 00:59:11.240
Do you want to go?

1253
00:59:11.340 --> 00:59:12.380
And I was like, I can't.

1254
00:59:12.460 --> 00:59:13.280
I'm going to WrestleMania.

1255
00:59:13.580 --> 00:59:16.400
And he was like, well, WrestleMania is also in Vegas and it's the same weekend.

1256
00:59:17.000 --> 00:59:18.520
And I was like, crap.

1257
00:59:18.760 --> 00:59:21.500
And I was like, I realized in that moment, like I should do both things.

1258
00:59:21.620 --> 00:59:22.940
Like it's funnier to do both things.

1259
00:59:23.320 --> 00:59:26.700
So I embark on what I refer to in the book as the world's weirdest biathlon.

1260
00:59:27.160 --> 00:59:30.800
Where on Saturday night, I go to a dead in company show and Sunday night I go to WrestleMania.

1261
00:59:31.780 --> 00:59:34.860
But then, you know, we were writing this book in 2024, 25.

1262
00:59:35.160 --> 00:59:38.440
And like it was impossible to write a book about fandom without including Taylor Swift.

1263
00:59:38.440 --> 00:59:42.740
My wife and I took our daughter and some of her friends to the Aero store.

1264
00:59:42.960 --> 00:59:43.320
We did the same.

1265
00:59:43.500 --> 00:59:43.720
Did you?

1266
00:59:43.900 --> 00:59:44.600
Yeah, both of us.

1267
00:59:44.700 --> 00:59:44.820
Yeah.

1268
00:59:45.140 --> 00:59:45.680
At SoFi?

1269
00:59:46.200 --> 00:59:47.580
I was in New Orleans.

1270
00:59:47.720 --> 00:59:47.960
Okay.

1271
00:59:48.140 --> 00:59:48.940
I was in Vancouver.

1272
00:59:48.940 --> 00:59:50.200
I was at her last show.

1273
00:59:50.440 --> 00:59:50.660
Yeah.

1274
00:59:50.680 --> 00:59:53.520
My wife and my daughter were also at her second to last show.

1275
00:59:53.660 --> 00:59:54.240
Oh, wow.

1276
00:59:54.320 --> 00:59:54.580
Yeah.

1277
00:59:55.960 --> 01:00:01.220
But my wife and her friend also went, her friend from college, and their daughters came.

1278
01:00:01.460 --> 01:00:05.200
And my wife's best friend made one of those photo books about it.

1279
01:00:05.360 --> 01:00:11.120
And the title of the book is Better Than Our Weddings because they both agreed that evening was better than each of their weddings.

1280
01:00:13.140 --> 01:00:14.900
They're like, it was more joyous.

1281
01:00:14.900 --> 01:00:15.080
Yeah.

1282
01:00:15.200 --> 01:00:16.140
I was happier.

1283
01:00:16.140 --> 01:00:16.520
Yeah.

1284
01:00:16.580 --> 01:00:17.920
It was more important in my life.

1285
01:00:18.100 --> 01:00:18.440
Like, yeah.

1286
01:00:18.900 --> 01:00:22.320
And Joe had taken his daughter to a Taylor Swift concert in North Carolina.

1287
01:00:22.320 --> 01:00:25.060
And so we were like, well, we have to write about Taylor Swift.

1288
01:00:25.300 --> 01:00:29.620
But what are two, like, 50-year-old dudes going to say about it that will matter?

1289
01:00:30.220 --> 01:00:30.460
Nothing.

1290
01:00:30.880 --> 01:00:32.560
But our daughters should do this.

1291
01:00:32.640 --> 01:00:34.780
So I had my daughter, Ivy, who's 14.

1292
01:00:35.260 --> 01:00:38.320
I was like, just write a chapter about what Taylor Swift means to you.

1293
01:00:38.480 --> 01:00:39.940
And his daughter did the same.

1294
01:00:39.940 --> 01:00:41.120
And then we put them together.

1295
01:00:41.120 --> 01:00:42.840
And it was like, and we did not edit them.

1296
01:00:43.000 --> 01:00:43.860
They are unedited.

1297
01:00:44.040 --> 01:00:45.380
They are like, this is what she wrote.

1298
01:00:45.540 --> 01:00:46.620
I added a few commas.

1299
01:00:46.760 --> 01:00:47.300
But other than that.

1300
01:00:47.740 --> 01:00:52.240
And so it's just a great little snapshot in time of, like, what Taylor Swift means to girls.

1301
01:00:52.320 --> 01:00:54.140
At different ages in the year 2024.

1302
01:00:54.800 --> 01:00:58.300
So we tried to cover not just sports, but all fandom.

1303
01:00:58.420 --> 01:01:00.260
I wrote a long section on TV fandom, too.

1304
01:01:00.460 --> 01:01:04.160
And wrote about, like, what I've observed about why TV matters to people.

1305
01:01:04.220 --> 01:01:11.420
And what is it about sort of bonding with characters on long-running TV shows that makes them feel like they matter in their lives and that sort of thing.

1306
01:01:11.480 --> 01:01:16.100
So it's really an attempt to be comprehensive about fandom as a phenomenon.

1307
01:01:16.380 --> 01:01:20.060
And you have some great pictures in here, too, from different events that you guys went to.

1308
01:01:20.060 --> 01:01:22.000
Yeah, there's some good WrestleMania pictures.

1309
01:01:22.320 --> 01:01:23.580
We were very close to the ring.

1310
01:01:23.700 --> 01:01:25.840
And I'm still not a wrestling fan.

1311
01:01:25.960 --> 01:01:31.060
But holy moly, those athletes are doing some crazy stuff, man.

1312
01:01:31.400 --> 01:01:31.900
Yeah, their bodies.

1313
01:01:32.100 --> 01:01:32.940
They're flying.

1314
01:01:33.060 --> 01:01:36.480
Like, the thing I write in the book is, like, wrestling might be fake.

1315
01:01:36.640 --> 01:01:40.020
But, like, the sounds their bodies make when they hit the mat are not.

1316
01:01:40.180 --> 01:01:42.980
Like, they are really jumping and flying and twisting.

1317
01:01:43.220 --> 01:01:44.740
And it's really wild.

1318
01:01:44.740 --> 01:01:52.540
I went with my friend Jen Statsky, who wrote with me on Parks and Rec and The Good Place and then created Hacks because she's an enormous wrestling fan.

1319
01:01:52.760 --> 01:01:59.720
And Eleanor Shellstrop, Kristen Bell's character, in The Good Place is a huge wrestling fan and is, like, deeply in love with Stone Cold Steve Austin.

1320
01:01:59.980 --> 01:02:01.560
And that all came from Jen.

1321
01:02:01.680 --> 01:02:03.020
That was all Jen's thing.

1322
01:02:03.620 --> 01:02:08.500
And I asked Jen to come with me to, like, be my, you know, Sherpa, to, like, walk me through it.

1323
01:02:08.500 --> 01:02:11.620
And the thing she said to me, I was, like, help me understand this.

1324
01:02:11.680 --> 01:02:13.340
Like, I don't, I can't access this.

1325
01:02:14.380 --> 01:02:15.780
It's, like, not a real sport.

1326
01:02:15.780 --> 01:02:17.220
And she goes, no, that's the whole point.

1327
01:02:17.380 --> 01:02:22.860
She goes, the thing that's great about wrestling is that it's the perfect marriage of sports and theater.

1328
01:02:23.360 --> 01:02:30.240
And when you think of it as the intersection of sports and theater, you can figure out why it's so compelling.

1329
01:02:30.780 --> 01:02:33.100
And I have to say, like, it was the perfect thing to say to me.

1330
01:02:33.100 --> 01:02:38.720
It was, like, one, I changed my frame of reference to, like, this isn't really a sport, to, like, it's not supposed to be.

1331
01:02:38.800 --> 01:02:44.320
It's supposed to be a dramatic performance done by the world's best athletes.

1332
01:02:44.320 --> 01:02:49.280
And that really helped me, like, get to the heart of why people are so nutty for it, you know?

1333
01:02:49.800 --> 01:02:56.340
It's funny that you mention not being a fan of something and then, like, getting absorbed by it.

1334
01:02:56.340 --> 01:03:02.120
So, I was in New York this year when the Knicks were in the playoffs.

1335
01:03:02.460 --> 01:03:04.460
What is the thing you win when you...

1336
01:03:04.460 --> 01:03:04.940
The championship.

1337
01:03:05.180 --> 01:03:05.800
The championship.

1338
01:03:05.900 --> 01:03:07.220
Just the NBA championship.

1339
01:03:07.280 --> 01:03:07.720
Yes, correct.

1340
01:03:08.080 --> 01:03:09.880
I'm like, does it have a name like the Stanley Cup?

1341
01:03:10.060 --> 01:03:10.680
I don't think so.

1342
01:03:10.900 --> 01:03:11.900
So, here's the thing.

1343
01:03:12.040 --> 01:03:16.400
I have never watched a basketball game on television in my life.

1344
01:03:16.540 --> 01:03:16.700
Right.

1345
01:03:16.860 --> 01:03:18.320
I've been to one Lakers game.

1346
01:03:18.320 --> 01:03:19.140
I took my son.

1347
01:03:19.580 --> 01:03:21.520
I don't particularly care for basketball.

1348
01:03:21.780 --> 01:03:25.400
My opinion is that, like, you could just turn it on in the last three minutes.

1349
01:03:25.400 --> 01:03:28.580
You don't need to watch the whole thing because it just goes back and forth the whole game.

1350
01:03:28.580 --> 01:03:30.460
And it's just sort of like, well, I'll just watch the end.

1351
01:03:31.160 --> 01:03:33.280
So, haven't ever been a fan of basketball.

1352
01:03:34.460 --> 01:03:37.260
I spent seven days in New York during this time.

1353
01:03:37.520 --> 01:03:43.780
And by that last night when they won, I was at a neighborhood watch party on the street.

1354
01:03:43.860 --> 01:03:44.400
She was outside.

1355
01:03:45.080 --> 01:03:45.760
At a bar.

1356
01:03:45.960 --> 01:03:47.700
Like, a bar had, like, a TV.

1357
01:03:48.180 --> 01:03:48.320
Yeah.

1358
01:03:48.320 --> 01:03:50.680
And I was like, oh, my God.

1359
01:03:51.080 --> 01:03:52.760
If they don't f***ing win!

1360
01:03:53.160 --> 01:03:54.440
They deserve it!

1361
01:03:54.640 --> 01:03:56.120
It's been 50 years!

1362
01:03:56.420 --> 01:03:58.220
Like, I was so hardcore.

1363
01:03:58.620 --> 01:03:59.620
And then they won.

1364
01:03:59.620 --> 01:04:01.360
And I was, like, on the street.

1365
01:04:01.420 --> 01:04:02.880
And it, like, erupted.

1366
01:04:02.880 --> 01:04:04.240
And cars are honking.

1367
01:04:04.240 --> 01:04:08.700
And the guy's got a giant blow-up basketball that we're all, like, kicking down the street.

1368
01:04:08.880 --> 01:04:09.020
Yeah.

1369
01:04:09.220 --> 01:04:11.520
And I was like, oh, my God.

1370
01:04:11.540 --> 01:04:11.760
Yeah.

1371
01:04:11.760 --> 01:04:12.780
I didn't even know.

1372
01:04:13.020 --> 01:04:13.660
I know.

1373
01:04:13.660 --> 01:04:16.000
It is deeply inside us, fandom.

1374
01:04:16.260 --> 01:04:17.860
That's one of the things that we really learned.

1375
01:04:18.340 --> 01:04:24.460
Is, like, you can go from, I don't care about this at all, to, if Jalen Brunson doesn't win

1376
01:04:24.460 --> 01:04:28.860
the MVP of the finals, I will throw myself in front of a train in, like, a second.

1377
01:04:29.060 --> 01:04:29.300
Yes!

1378
01:04:29.300 --> 01:04:35.360
And the most sort of interesting part of this whole experience was we talked to this professor

1379
01:04:35.360 --> 01:04:37.300
who studies sports fandom.

1380
01:04:37.440 --> 01:04:38.320
His name is Daniel Wan.

1381
01:04:38.500 --> 01:04:40.540
He teaches at Murray State University.

1382
01:04:40.540 --> 01:04:46.100
And the thing he said to us that rang so true was, look, if sports were just, like, fun

1383
01:04:46.100 --> 01:04:49.780
and a good way to kill a couple hours, that would be fine.

1384
01:04:49.780 --> 01:04:52.780
But it wouldn't explain that reaction you had.

1385
01:04:52.900 --> 01:04:57.460
And it wouldn't explain why a billion people in the world care about it so deeply.

1386
01:04:57.640 --> 01:05:03.040
And he said, in order for that to be true, it must be the case that sports fandom, and

1387
01:05:03.040 --> 01:05:07.520
fandom in general, is satisfying some kind of deep emotional need.

1388
01:05:07.700 --> 01:05:10.400
Like, an emotional need is being met by this phenomenon.

1389
01:05:10.820 --> 01:05:13.900
And that's why it erupts out of us the way it does.

1390
01:05:14.000 --> 01:05:18.620
It's because it's drilling down into our souls and meeting us at some very deep and primal

1391
01:05:18.620 --> 01:05:19.020
place.

1392
01:05:19.620 --> 01:05:24.980
And that feeling that you just described, so my son is a huge sports fan, but has never

1393
01:05:24.980 --> 01:05:25.580
liked soccer.

1394
01:05:25.800 --> 01:05:27.600
I've tried to get him to watch with me.

1395
01:05:27.680 --> 01:05:28.340
He doesn't care.

1396
01:05:28.480 --> 01:05:29.660
He thinks it's boring.

1397
01:05:29.880 --> 01:05:32.140
There's not enough scoring, all those things that people think.

1398
01:05:32.640 --> 01:05:35.160
He was out with his friends on the first night of the World Cup.

1399
01:05:35.460 --> 01:05:37.780
And he was like, what do you guys want to do?

1400
01:05:37.780 --> 01:05:42.480
And they were like, there's a watch party in Koreatown because South Korea is playing

1401
01:05:42.480 --> 01:05:43.060
in the World Cup.

1402
01:05:43.220 --> 01:05:44.280
And he was like, all right.

1403
01:05:44.720 --> 01:05:49.080
And that, all of a sudden, I started getting these texts that was like, this is low-key.

1404
01:05:49.660 --> 01:05:50.900
Lock-in, dad, low-key.

1405
01:05:50.920 --> 01:05:53.220
This is the most incredible thing I've ever seen.

1406
01:05:53.340 --> 01:06:00.740
And it's my son and his friends and like a thousand Koreans in at like an outdoor park

1407
01:06:00.740 --> 01:06:02.820
in Koreatown watching the World Cup.

1408
01:06:02.940 --> 01:06:07.320
And when they scored a goal, he was like, I've never felt anything like this in my life.

1409
01:06:07.420 --> 01:06:09.000
It was like a jolt of electricity.

1410
01:06:09.000 --> 01:06:13.660
And like that night, he's staying up until three in the morning, like learning about

1411
01:06:13.660 --> 01:06:18.740
like all the players on the team, where they play, what the European leagues are, like learning

1412
01:06:18.740 --> 01:06:22.240
about like the transfer system in soccer, which is incredibly complicated.

1413
01:06:22.560 --> 01:06:26.660
And now he's just got World Cup fever and he's like living and dying with all these matches.

1414
01:06:26.660 --> 01:06:29.380
And I just think it speaks to something.

1415
01:06:29.940 --> 01:06:35.980
There's a lot of reasons why fandom occurs in people, but like life is a little bit lonely

1416
01:06:36.680 --> 01:06:40.660
and sports creates communities that we can feel like we're a part of.

1417
01:06:40.760 --> 01:06:44.620
And like you're standing on the street in New York City, suddenly feeling like nothing has

1418
01:06:44.620 --> 01:06:50.120
ever mattered more to me in my life than this thing that I did not care about one week ago.

1419
01:06:50.600 --> 01:06:51.020
Exactly.

1420
01:06:51.420 --> 01:06:58.220
I mean, on game four, we walked to Central Park and we watched the game like through some trees.

1421
01:06:58.700 --> 01:07:02.700
They had the Central Park watch party, but it was like, it was at Wolman Rink.

1422
01:07:02.820 --> 01:07:04.580
And so it was like kind of a closed thing.

1423
01:07:04.580 --> 01:07:07.400
And we're like, we're standing on some rocks.

1424
01:07:07.620 --> 01:07:09.220
And I'm like, who am I?

1425
01:07:09.520 --> 01:07:12.400
I didn't even know this team existed four days ago.

1426
01:07:12.520 --> 01:07:13.160
Yeah, I know.

1427
01:07:13.820 --> 01:07:18.280
I'll tell you when I fall apart, it doesn't matter what sports I'm watching.

1428
01:07:18.280 --> 01:07:19.280
I love sports.

1429
01:07:19.440 --> 01:07:22.940
I quickly just glom on to whatever everyone, like, what are we watching?

1430
01:07:22.980 --> 01:07:24.060
Oh, we're watching World Cup.

1431
01:07:24.180 --> 01:07:24.300
Great.

1432
01:07:24.440 --> 01:07:24.560
Okay.

1433
01:07:24.780 --> 01:07:25.180
Oh, the champion.

1434
01:07:25.320 --> 01:07:25.420
Okay.

1435
01:07:25.840 --> 01:07:26.000
Whatever.

1436
01:07:26.400 --> 01:07:28.100
I just glom on and I love it.

1437
01:07:34.820 --> 01:07:42.120
And you see the different emotions go across the faces of thousands of people who are sharing this moment with each other.

1438
01:07:42.360 --> 01:07:42.480
Yeah.

1439
01:07:43.000 --> 01:07:44.720
I, like, cry.

1440
01:07:45.600 --> 01:07:46.300
Every time.

1441
01:07:46.480 --> 01:07:47.240
I cry every time.

1442
01:07:47.280 --> 01:07:47.780
Every time.

1443
01:07:47.780 --> 01:07:49.440
I didn't know anything about Jalen.

1444
01:07:49.640 --> 01:07:50.800
I call him by his first name now.

1445
01:07:50.800 --> 01:07:58.560
And I just was like, oh, like, hearing his life story, I'm just like, and then, you know, you see his mom and I'm just like, forget it.

1446
01:07:58.740 --> 01:08:00.160
Like, it's amazing.

1447
01:08:00.340 --> 01:08:00.520
Yeah.

1448
01:08:00.680 --> 01:08:01.120
It's amazing.

1449
01:08:01.360 --> 01:08:01.980
You're right.

1450
01:08:02.180 --> 01:08:04.900
That shared grief is part of it, too.

1451
01:08:05.040 --> 01:08:06.640
Like, it's not just the winning.

1452
01:08:06.920 --> 01:08:09.020
It's failures and triumphs.

1453
01:08:09.020 --> 01:08:12.260
And the reason, like, New York would have gone crazy no matter what,

1454
01:08:12.420 --> 01:08:21.180
but 53 years means that there are whole generations of people who have never known their favorite team to be a winner at the end of the year.

1455
01:08:21.380 --> 01:08:26.540
The most extreme example of this in my life is I'm a Red Sox fan, and they went 86 years.

1456
01:08:26.540 --> 01:08:35.240
So, 32 more years, 33 more years than the Knicks did without winning, and in that intervening time, sort of like the Knicks, had a bunch of near misses,

1457
01:08:35.480 --> 01:08:40.640
like a bunch of close calls and tragic failures and embarrassing errors made at the wrong time.

1458
01:08:41.380 --> 01:08:49.680
And when they finally won in 2004, there was an article written, many articles written, about the celebration that happened.

1459
01:08:49.680 --> 01:08:57.380
And there was a story that I think about all the time, which is at 3 in the morning, a guy had a thought, which was, I need to share this with my dad.

1460
01:08:57.380 --> 01:09:00.820
And his dad had passed away many years earlier and had never seen them win.

1461
01:09:00.940 --> 01:09:04.740
He had lived his entire life in a world in which they had never won the World Series.

1462
01:09:05.420 --> 01:09:12.160
And his dad was buried at Mount Auburn Cemetery outside Boston, and he got a bottle of champagne and a Red Sox hat.

1463
01:09:12.220 --> 01:09:13.640
And he was like, I'm going to go to my dad's grave.

1464
01:09:13.960 --> 01:09:18.280
I'm going to leave him this bottle of champagne and this Red Sox hat and just celebrate with him.

1465
01:09:18.840 --> 01:09:22.780
And he was walking towards the cemetery, and he was thinking, like, how am I going to get in?

1466
01:09:22.920 --> 01:09:23.860
Like, it's 3 in the morning.

1467
01:09:24.060 --> 01:09:25.400
Like, there's a gate.

1468
01:09:25.580 --> 01:09:26.020
It's locked.

1469
01:09:26.420 --> 01:09:30.520
And as he was walking, he started to see other people walking in the same direction.

1470
01:09:31.040 --> 01:09:32.360
He got to the cemetery.

1471
01:09:32.460 --> 01:09:37.940
The guy who was the night guard at the cemetery had anticipated this and had unlocked the gate and opened it.

1472
01:09:38.120 --> 01:09:45.400
He walked to his dad's grave, put the hat and the champagne down, and saw, like, 11 other people just in his line of vision

1473
01:09:46.000 --> 01:09:49.040
speaking out loud to people in their graves.

1474
01:09:49.040 --> 01:09:50.180
This is making me tear up.

1475
01:09:50.240 --> 01:09:50.620
I know.

1476
01:09:50.820 --> 01:09:51.960
And it's beautiful.

1477
01:09:51.960 --> 01:09:53.980
It's so important and meaningful.

1478
01:09:54.240 --> 01:10:00.520
And if you think of sports as, like, a distraction, a diversion from the banality of everyday life, that's fine.

1479
01:10:00.520 --> 01:10:02.280
That is what they are at some level.

1480
01:10:02.280 --> 01:10:10.000
But when you have communities that bond around these teams and the fandom is passed down from generation to generation,

1481
01:10:10.340 --> 01:10:16.700
and important moments and memories are made alongside those teams and alongside those moments,

1482
01:10:17.340 --> 01:10:19.040
then it becomes something else.

1483
01:10:19.200 --> 01:10:27.580
Then it becomes a thing where, like, you are not thinking about necessarily, like, OG and an OB's incredible tip in in game four.

1484
01:10:27.580 --> 01:10:34.080
What you're thinking about is your grandmother who used to sit in her living room and watch Knicks games and root for them

1485
01:10:34.080 --> 01:10:39.820
and who passed away 22 years ago and how much she would have loved this moment.

1486
01:10:40.120 --> 01:10:43.900
And it connects you to her and it connects you to your kids and to your friends.

1487
01:10:44.120 --> 01:10:50.780
And it really is – there's a great quote from the pope of all people, not this current pope,

1488
01:10:50.880 --> 01:10:56.780
but I think it was John Paul II who said about soccer, calling it football as Europeans do,

1489
01:10:56.780 --> 01:11:02.000
he said, of all of the unimportant things, soccer is the most important.

1490
01:11:02.920 --> 01:11:05.940
Like, and it's – that's how I think we should think of these things.

1491
01:11:05.980 --> 01:11:13.900
It's like, yes, of course it's not as important as, like, a Supreme Court decision or a federal election or the eradication of a disease.

1492
01:11:14.540 --> 01:11:20.220
But it is important and it's important in a way that really does matter to an enormous number of people.

1493
01:11:20.380 --> 01:11:22.060
And that's why we wanted to write the book.

1494
01:11:22.440 --> 01:11:23.400
That's so great.

1495
01:11:23.840 --> 01:11:26.240
Well, I think people are going to love your book.

1496
01:11:26.240 --> 01:11:30.520
You know, we're so thankful for our office community and the Office Ladies fans

1497
01:11:30.520 --> 01:11:33.660
and the fandom and family that we have found with them.

1498
01:11:34.040 --> 01:11:35.920
And you guys, we love Mike Schur.

1499
01:11:36.020 --> 01:11:36.880
We know you do, too.

1500
01:11:37.320 --> 01:11:38.420
You're going to love his book.

1501
01:11:38.780 --> 01:11:40.360
I'm going to put a link to it in our stories.

1502
01:11:40.700 --> 01:11:40.800
Thank you.

1503
01:11:41.240 --> 01:11:42.100
Hope you like it.

1504
01:11:42.360 --> 01:11:43.960
Mike, thanks for joining us today.

1505
01:11:43.960 --> 01:11:45.260
It is my absolute pleasure.

1506
01:11:45.440 --> 01:11:46.240
It's lovely to see you.

1507
01:11:46.260 --> 01:11:49.980
And your generosity and talking all things office and all things sports and fandom.

1508
01:11:50.200 --> 01:11:51.580
I love talking about the office.

1509
01:11:51.700 --> 01:11:52.340
I'll talk about it forever.

1510
01:11:52.580 --> 01:11:54.920
Anytime you want me to come talk about the office, I'll come back.

1511
01:11:54.920 --> 01:11:55.860
Okay, great.

1512
01:11:56.780 --> 01:11:58.980
Well, we have two more years of our contracts.

1513
01:11:59.280 --> 01:11:59.720
There you go.

1514
01:12:00.640 --> 01:12:01.500
Well, good job.

1515
01:12:02.260 --> 01:12:02.940
Thank you.

1516
01:12:02.940 --> 01:12:04.160
Thanks so much.

1517
01:12:04.240 --> 01:12:05.160
That was wonderful.

1518
01:12:05.600 --> 01:12:06.060
That was so fun.

1519
